WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.476
A process holds 512 MB of memory, in pages of 4 KB.

2
00:00:07.476 --> 00:00:11.972
Its page table maps each page to a frame of RAM.

3
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:17.815
It calls fork, and the child is running 1.7 milliseconds later.

4
00:00:17.815 --> 00:00:20.415
Copying 512 MB would take 33.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:25.504
And copying every page would need a second 512 MB of RAM.

6
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.889
Instead, the child gets its own page table, pointing at the parent's frames.

7
00:00:30.889 --> 00:00:32.732
That's about 1 MB of table.

8
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.043
But then a write by the parent would show up in the child.

9
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:40.385
So the kernel marks every entry read-only, in both tables.

10
00:00:40.385 --> 00:00:41.592
Reads still work.

11
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.820
When the child writes page 1, the CPU refuses, and faults into the kernel.

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.619
The kernel copies just that page to a free frame, points the child at the copy, and makes it writable.

13
00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:55.696
That takes about 2 microseconds.

14
00:00:55.833 --> 00:01:01.279
Frame B now has one map, so the parent's next write there just gets write access back.

15
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:02.609
That's copy-on-write.

16
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.610
But fork promised the child memory the kernel never set aside.

17
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.971
An honest kernel would refuse: a 4 GB JVM on an 8 GB machine couldn't fork at all.

18
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.680
So Linux overcommits.

19
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.174
It says yes, and bets that most promises are never collected.

20
00:01:20.174 --> 00:01:22.900
A request for 100 GB returns at once.

21
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:25.466
Then the batch job touches its pages.

22
00:01:25.466 --> 00:01:29.032
Each first touch takes a real frame, until RAM is full.

23
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:33.465
The next touch faults, with no frame left and no error to return.

24
00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:37.631
The kernel drops cached file pages, and the job fills those too.

25
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.886
So the out-of-memory killer scores each process by the memory it holds.

26
00:01:42.886 --> 00:01:44.533
The JVM is the biggest.

27
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.822
It gets SIGKILL, with no chance to clean up.

28
00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:51.796
The batch job asked for the last page, but the JVM died.

29
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:54.723
fork copies only the pages that get written.

30
00:01:54.723 --> 00:02:00.236
The kernel promises more than it has, and when the bill comes, the biggest process pays.
